Letting $x=-5$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{3(-5+1)^n}{n\cdot4^n}
 &= \displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{3(-4)^n}{n\cdot4^n}
 \\\\
 &= \displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{3(-1)^n(4)^n}{n\cdot4^n}
 \\\\
&=3 \displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n}
\end{align}$

This is a multiple of the alternating harmonic series, which is known to converge.

Letting $x=3$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{3(3+1)^n}{n\cdot4^n}
 &= \displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{3(4)^n}{n\cdot4^n}
 \\\\
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{3}{n} \\\\
 &=3 \displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{1}{n}
\end{align}$

This is a multiple of the harmonic series, which is known to diverge.
